                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_process:       `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> About 60% of your 2.6 Mt is process emissions inherent to clinker — no electricity switch touches it, and capture is years away. Naming what is cheap-and-ready versus structurally hard is what lets you sequence the budget instead of chasing a slogan.`,
                    coach_r1_electricity:   `<strong>Clean electricity won't solve this.</strong> Roughly 60% of your emissions are process emissions from the chemistry of making clinker — they exist no matter how green your power is. Mis-diagnose the problem here and you will mis-spend the CA$600M later.`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>“It's all addressable” is the slogan, not the chemistry.</strong> Process emissions from clinker are structurally hard and capture is still a pilot. Set net-zero on faith and you will commit to a number your technology can't yet deliver — the over-promise trap, three rounds early.`,
                    coach_r1_activist:      `<strong>Satisfying only the 4.8% is how you mobilize the other pole.</strong> Win the activist with speed and your dividend-focused base — 60% of your register — starts talking to a different slate. A polarized field punishes the leader who treats one camp as the whole board.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>A “sound technical plan” does not speak for itself in a polarized field.</strong> The union, the province, the NGOs and two investor camps all read the same plan differently. Underestimate the polarization now and the AGM will teach it to you the hard way.`,
                    coach_r1_rush:          `<strong>A bold target this quarter, before you've diagnosed or consulted, is the announcement trap.</strong> The market reads an unfunded promise as a future dividend cut and the stock wobbles on uncertainty alone. Sequencing — not speed — is what holds a polarized coalition.`,
                    coach_r1_stall:         `<strong>Saying as little as possible reads as no answer.</strong> In a polarized field silence invites everyone to assume the worst — the activist escalates and the carbon bill compounds while you wait. A two-quarter mandate is not a licence to drift.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp opening.</strong> You named the hard process emissions, read the field as genuinely polarized, and framed a sequenced transition rather than a slogan. That is the posture that funds the cheap wins first and keeps every camp at the table for the harder calls to come.`,
                    coach_r2_macc:          `<strong>That is MACC discipline.</strong> You loaded the cheap, ready tonnes — alternative fuels and clinker substitution — staged electrification, and kept capture a small contingent pilot. You bought the most carbon per dollar, and the plan reads as credible rather than symbolic.`,
                    coach_r2_capture:       `<strong>You over-funded the carbon-capture pilot.</strong> Capture abates a tonne at CA$200+ and is still unproven, while alternative fuels abate at CA$40 and are ready now. A quarter of the budget on the most expensive, least mature option is optics — your MACC says fund the cheap, ready tonnes first.`,
                    coach_r2_even:          `<strong>You split the budget evenly across all four levers.</strong> That funds carbon capture at CA$200+/t at the same level as alternative fuels at CA$40/t. Even distribution feels fair, but the MACC is not a democracy — it ranks by cost per tonne abated.`,
                    coach_r2_under:         `<strong>You left most of the budget unspent.</strong> The carbon bill compounds toward CA$440M a year; under-investing now defers abatement and lets the liability grow. The CA$600M is there to buy down a balance-sheet risk, not to be hoarded.`,
                    coach_r4_committed:     `<strong>Committing only to what you can deliver is the discipline that survives.</strong> Hard targets where the MACC and the just transition back them, capture-dependent cuts framed as ambitions — that's a plan you can defend three quarters from now when a pilot underperforms. Credibility is built by not over-promising.`,
                    coach_r3_idle:          `<strong>Idling the kiln cut 600,000 t overnight — and 480 jobs in a town where you're 30% of employment.</strong> The union has called a work stoppage at your other Québec plants in solidarity and the premier just called the board. Your emissions are down; your operations are halted. What did the “just” in just transition require here?`,
                    coach_r3_unionignore:   `<strong>Running the kiln and saying nothing defers the hardest question.</strong> The union and the province both know the oldest kiln is the carbon problem and the jobs problem at once. Silence isn't neutrality — it tells them you have no plan for the people the transition touches first.`,
                    coach_r3_esg:           `<strong>Going all-in for the ESG camp mobilizes the other pole.</strong> Your dividend base reads an aggressive target as a dividend cut and the stock drops; now your value investors are talking to the same activist about a different slate. Satisfying one pole carelessly is how you lose both.`,
                    coach_r3_value:         `<strong>Reassuring only the dividend base soft-pedals the transition the activist demanded.</strong> The carbon liability doesn't shrink because you under-sold the plan — you've simply given the ESG camp and the activist a credible grievance to take to the AGM.`,
                    coach_r3_govonly:       `<strong>Courting the premier while leaving the NGOs to complain is a half-coalition.</strong> A low-power NGO becomes high-impact through the media and the courts — an unmanaged greenwashing claim can drag your share price and your government relationship with it. Engage them on verification.`,
                    coach_r3_dismiss:       `<strong>You dismissed the NGOs as noise.</strong> They've now filed a complaint over your “net-zero” marketing and the story is national news, dragging the share price and the provincial relationship with it. A stakeholder you can't control still needs managing — through verification, not dismissal.`,
                    coach_r3_divcut:        `<strong>Cutting the dividend to fund capex confirms the dividend base's worst fear.</strong> A register that is 60% income-focused doesn't wait to be convinced — the stock falls hard and the activist's dividend-cut headline writes itself. Sequenced capex was meant to avoid exactly this.`,
                    coach_r3_divsilent:     `<strong>You never addressed the dividend.</strong> Your base is 60% income-focused and in a polarized field silence is read as the bad answer — the market assumes the worst and the stock falls on uncertainty alone. Investor communication is part of the transition, not a footnote.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You committed publicly to a 50% cut by 2030 and net-zero by 2035.</strong> Three quarters later the capture pilot underperforms, you miss the interim milestone, and now you face a credibility crisis <em>and</em> a greenwashing accusation from the NGOs you tried to please. Which targets should have been ambitions, not commitments?`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>Committing to nothing specific satisfies no one.</strong> “A journey to net-zero” with no interim milestones tells the activist you're stalling and the ESG camp you're not serious. Credible doesn't mean reckless — it means real numbers where you can deliver them.`,
                    coach_r4_hide:          `<strong>Hiding the messy interim metrics buys one good quarter and a credibility problem.</strong> The quarter you finally have to disclose a miss, the market re-rates you for the surprise as much as the number. Consistent, audited reporting is what lets a multi-year plan survive a bad quarter.`,
                    coach_r4_optics:        `<strong>Choosing the flattering metric each quarter is how boards stop trusting a transition.</strong> Inconsistent reporting reads as something to hide — and the activist will build the proxy case on exactly that. Report the same set every quarter, good or bad.`,
